<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- MainActivity's windowBackground — brand blue (@color/splash_background)
     with the Saveur logo centered at a fixed, known size we fully control.
     Deliberately NOT
     using the system SplashScreen API's windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon for
     this logo (see Theme.App.SplashScreen in styles.xml, which now sets no
     icon at all) — that mechanism was tried twice with well-padded assets
     and still rendered the logo huge/cropped on-device, seemingly ignoring
     the source PNG's own transparent padding. A plain windowBackground
     drawable has no such platform-side auto-sizing: this <item>'s
     width/height directly control the on-screen box.

     Product request ("I want the splash screen to be like [reference]...
     The saveur logo and the app name will be on the same horizontal
     level") — was the solo "S" mark alone (@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground,
     the app icon's own foreground layer). This Android launch background is
     a static drawable (no runtime text layout the way the iOS storyboards'
     Auto Layout row can do — see LaunchScreen.storyboard/BootSplash.
     storyboard's own matching comments for that side), so there's no way to
     lay a live text label out next to the mark here; @drawable/
     splash_logo_wordmark is a single pre-rendered PNG (drawable-mdpi/
     -hdpi/-xhdpi/-xxhdpi/-xxxhdpi, generated from this same logo mark +
     the app's own MontserratAlternates-Black wordmark font, matching the
     iOS storyboards' lockup) with the mark and "Saveur" already composited
     side by side.

     BUG FIX, ROUND 4 (real-device report: STILL huge, cut off on the right
     edge with a visible gap on the left — an ASYMMETRIC overflow pattern,
     not the symmetric "too big but centered" pattern round 3's gravity fix
     should have produced if it had actually taken effect. Two real issues,
     both addressed here:
       1. The density-bucketed assets were regenerated at 402x100dp
          (mdpi 402x100px ... xxxhdpi 1607x400px) — on a typical ~360-412dp
          wide phone, a 402dp-WIDE logo is already at or beyond full screen
          width by design, before any XML rendering question even applies.
          Re-exported all five density buckets from the highest-res source
          at a smaller 240x60dp base (mdpi 240x60px, hdpi 360x90px, xhdpi
          480x120px, xxhdpi 720x180px, xxxhdpi 960x240px — same aspect
          ratio, downsampled with high-quality resampling, not just
          shrunk in XML) so the logo reads as a normal splash mark with
          breathing room either side, not a screen-filling banner.
       2. The asymmetric (left-gap/right-cut) overflow pattern in the
          report doesn't match what round 3's <bitmap android:gravity=
          "center"> fix should produce even in a worst case — it matches
          the OLDER, pre-round-3 stretched-drawable behavior instead,
          which is the signature of a STALE BUILD (an old APK still
          installed, or an incremental build that didn't actually pick up
          the changed resource) rather than a fourth wrong theory about
          the XML itself. If this still looks the same after rebuilding
          with these new, visibly smaller assets, that on its own confirms
          it's a build/install caching issue, not this drawable — a clean
          rebuild (uninstall the app from the device first, then
          `cd android && ./gradlew clean`, then a fresh install) is the
          next step either way.
     <bitmap> (a distinct drawable type from a plain PNG/drawable
     reference) is kept from round 3: BitmapDrawable implements its own
     gravity-based draw() that positions content at intrinsic size within
     its bounds without stretching to fill, unlike a bare drawable
     reference used directly on an <item>. -->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item android:drawable="@color/splash_background" />
    <item>
        <bitmap
            android:src="@drawable/splash_logo_wordmark"
            android:gravity="center" />
    </item>
</layer-list>
